<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=helicopter">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helicopter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as chopper</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A helicopter is a rotorcraft whose lift and thrust come from horizontally spinning rotors, so that it can take off and land vertically and hover. Chinese children played with bamboo flying toys from around 400 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> designed an 'aerial screw' in the early 1480s. In 1907 the Breguet brothers' Gyroplane No. 1 lifted its pilot about 0.6 metres while men held it steady, and on 13 November 1907 Paul Cornu's machine made the first free flight with a pilot, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Focke-Wulf Fw 61 of 1936 was the first practical helicopter, and Igor Sikorsky's R-4 of 1942 the first in full-scale production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helicopter.jpg" alt="Helicopter" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fly45yt · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VH-SUF_Taking_Off.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The fourth-century Daoist book Baopuzi by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ge-hong">Ge Hong</a> reportedly describes some of the ideas inherent to rotary-wing aircraft. Designs similar to the Chinese toy appeared in some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> paintings, and in the eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries Western scientists developed flying machines based on it. The word helicopter is adapted from the French hélicoptère, coined by Gustave Ponton d'Amécourt in 1861 from the Ancient Greek helix, spiral, and pteron, wing.</p><p>Engines held back the early machines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">Rubber</a> bands and similar devices limited them to toys and small models, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engines</a> lacked the power for manned flight. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">internal combustion engine</a> at the end of the nineteenth century made lifting humans possible. In the early 1920s the Argentine Raúl Pateras-Pescara de Castelluccio demonstrated one of the first successful applications of cyclic pitch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A helicopter's rotor consists of a mast, hub and blades. The torque of a single main rotor must be countered, and the design Igor Sikorsky settled on for his VS-300 was a smaller tail rotor at the end of a tail boom. Between 1939 and 1943 the VS-300 went through four iterations and became the basis for modern helicopters. Twin-rotor designs carry more payload, and coaxial, tiltrotor and compound helicopters also fly. The pilot works the cyclic, the collective, the anti-torque pedals and the throttle, and hovering is the most demanding task. If the engine fails, the helicopter can enter autorotation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Heinrich Focke designed the transverse twin-rotor Fw 61, which first flew in June 1936. Hanna Reitsch flew it inside the Deutschlandhalle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in February 1938, and it set records between 1937 and 1939. On 11 December 1951 Charles Kaman's K-225 became the first turbine-powered helicopter. Because it can take off and land vertically and hover, a helicopter does work that was impossible or slow for other aircraft, including transport, military roles, firefighting, search and rescue, medical transport and aerial observation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helicopter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tanks broke through the gates of the presidential palace hours after the last Americans were lifted out by <b>helicopter</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-30">30 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>President Fernando de la Rúa resigned and fled the Casa Rosada by <b>helicopter</b> as protests over economic collapse left dozens dead.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-20">20 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Communist guerrillas in the Mekong Delta beat off a South Vietnamese attack backed by American <b>helicopters</b> and advisers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-2">2 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The design pioneered rotor technology later used in <b>helicopters</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-9">9 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It saw the first large-scale <b>helicopter</b> air assault and the first tactical use of B-52 bombers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-14">14 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1907</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Jacques Breguet · Louis Breguet · Paul Cornu</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Hermann Ganswindt's helicopter may have carried people in Berlin-Schöneberg in July 1901, but the film of the flight is lost; the Breguet machine was held steady by a man at each corner, so Cornu's flight of 13 November 1907 is reported as the first free one.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">Internal combustion engine<span>1794</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=helicopter">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
